package run

import "strings"

// writeDesignerAnimationGuidance appends the invariant authoring guidance that
// accompanies every backend-resolved Designer animation profile. The resolved
// profile remains the resource authority; this text turns its budgets into
// concrete hot-loop, frame-pacing, and lifecycle practices for the worker.
func writeDesignerAnimationGuidance(b *strings.Builder, profileID string) {
	if b == nil || strings.TrimSpace(profileID) == "" {
		return
	}
	b.WriteString("- animation runtime contract: use only the pinned local runtime(s); no CDN, remote imports, arbitrary package execution, or network access. Honor lifecycle and resource budgets, pause offscreen, stop when hidden, provide a static first frame for reduced motion, and clean up tickers, listeners, workers, geometries, materials, textures, and WebGL contexts. Use CSS/WAAPI/SVG for motion_ui, optional Three.js only for spatial_3d, dotLottie/Rive only for imported vector_playback, and MP4 playback for final_render. Import only assets the user owns or has licensed for the intended commercial use and redistribution; never source marketplace/community assets by default.\n")
	b.WriteString("- animation quality and frame-pacing contract: optimize for smooth display-refresh playback without hard-coding 60 FPS or claiming a measured frame rate without profiling evidence. Keep one authoritative animation scheduler; drive progress from monotonic timestamps rather than per-frame increments; clamp or rebase elapsed time after pauses so resume never jumps. For finite motion, settle on a static final frame and cancel the scheduler.\n")
	b.WriteString("- animation hot-loop contract: precompute and cache invariant geometry, paths, gradients, sprites, text metrics, and lookup data. Reuse buffers and objects; avoid per-frame allocation, DOM creation/removal, selector or layout reads, style-string churn, asset decoding, shader compilation, and repeated listener registration. Batch reads before writes. Prefer transform and opacity for DOM motion, avoid large paint-heavy animated blur/filter/box-shadow/clip-path effects, cap canvas resolution and device pixel ratio to the profile budgets, and reduce scene detail or update frequency when sustained frame time exceeds the available refresh budget.\n")
	b.WriteString("- animation publication and inspection contract: every text/html document declaring swarm.animation/v1 must carry the backend-resolved reviewed animation profile; never publish declared animation HTML through an unprofiled direct create. Managed animated HTML is successful only after the trusted manage_artifact publication call returns a ready HTML reference whose server-owned animation preflight passed runtime binding, artifact-owned live playback, exact seek, stable-pixel, and viewport-containment checks, plus exactly three `animation_inspection_references` carrying ready image/png references for the start, resolved-phrase/middle, and exit frames. The start inspection is exactly time_ms=0: render a substantive, intentional opening composition there with the concept's primary subject and any essential labels already visible and legible; never use a blank, near-black, empty-background, or fade-in-only prelude whose meaningful content appears later. Call media_inspect once on each complete exact image reference; never pass the text/html source reference to media_inspect. The final report must include compact evidence for all three frames and explicitly cover clipping/overflow, aspect ratio and object sizing, requested-element fidelity, text legibility, unintended overlaps, scrollbars/capture chrome, and fidelity to the brief. A failed publication preflight or any failed/missing representative-frame inspection is a failed slot; do not claim it as successful or let it consume a requested successful variant slot.\n")
	b.WriteString("- animation density semantics: report simulated_agents as the actual logical agent count advanced by the simulation, rendered_micro_motes as the actual independently rendered micro-particle count, and visual_density_target as the perceptual density goal rather than a measured count. Do not describe instancing, trails, glow, interpolation, or repeated visual samples as additional simulated agents. If a brief requests a large agent count, either implement that many simulated_agents or state the smaller actual count honestly while using rendered_micro_motes and visual_density_target only for their distinct meanings.\n")

	switch strings.TrimSpace(profileID) {
	case "motion_ui":
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui performance tips: use CSS/WAAPI transform and opacity for simple element motion, Canvas 2D for dense changing scenes, and SVG primarily for static or low-count vector structure. Do not mutate many DOM/SVG attributes or filters every frame. Size canvases from their CSS display size at the capped DPR, pre-render invariant layers or sprites to reusable buffers, and keep one requestAnimationFrame owner for Canvas work. The trusted renderer already captures the document at exactly 1920x1080, so author a direct fixed 1920x1080 stage at x=0/y=0 with no responsive scale/min(vw/1920,vh/1080) wrapper. The trusted viewport audit rejects every visible element or pseudo-element whose bounds leave that frame at representative timestamps: set html/body margin 0, width/height 100%, overflow hidden, and border-box sizing; use opacity, masks, inset clipping, or fully in-frame transforms for entrances/exits; keep shadows, glows, controls, and pseudo-elements inset from every edge.\n")
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui live-preview contract: an animation is not live merely because it implements swarm.animation/v1. That API is random-access capture control; Artifact Studio is not required to call seek() continuously. The artifact itself must start visibly animating when its managed preview document becomes visible and motion is allowed, without a Play button or other capture UI that embedding may hide.\n")
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui implementation recipe: author one pure renderAt(timeMs) function that renders any canonical timestamp. On DOMContentLoaded, render frame zero, then when reduced motion is not requested start exactly one requestAnimationFrame loop. In each tick derive time from performance.now() and a rebased epoch, call renderAt(timeMs), and schedule the next tick. On document.visibilitychange cancel the frame while hidden; when visible, rebase the epoch from the paused animation time before restarting so playback does not jump. For finite non-looping motion, clamp at duration_ms, render the final frame, and stop scheduling; for an explicitly looping concept, wrap time modulo duration_ms.\n")
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui deterministic-control recipe: in a parser-executed classic script in `head` (not a deferred, async, module, DOMContentLoaded, or dynamically inserted script), synchronously construct the swarm.animation/v1 runtime and publish it before DOMContentLoaded. Keep that parser-time bootstrap minimal and syntactically conservative: define renderAt and the runtime, bind it immediately, and defer DOM node lookup, Path2D/canvas/scene construction, font/layout work, and scheduler startup until ready()/DOMContentLoaded. Do not build a large scene before the bind, because any earlier parse-time exception or syntax error makes the trusted lifecycle indistinguishable from a missing runtime. If globalThis.__SWARM_ANIMATION_BIND__ is a function, call it immediately with the runtime and treat a false return as failure; do not assign globalThis.__SWARM_ANIMATION_V1__ because trusted preflight owns that immutable proxy. Otherwise, for standalone preview, assign the runtime to globalThis.__SWARM_ANIMATION_V1__. Do not defer either branch behind a promise, event, import, or asset load. Before publication, syntax-check the exact complete classic script and verify the bind statement is actually reached; never infer parser success merely because the source text contains __SWARM_ANIMATION_BIND__. ready() waits for fonts/assets/layout, calls renderAt(currentTimeMs), and returns exactly {duration_ms, fps}. seek(timeMs) clamps the requested time, pauses/cancels the live RAF if necessary, calls renderAt(timeMs), waits until that frame is stable, and returns exactly {time_ms: timeMs}; it must not start a second loop or set a flag that merely pretends playback exists. stop() cancels the live RAF and freezes the current frame. The live scheduler and ready()/seek()/stop() must share renderAt and the same timeline state; never rely on the host to advance time by repeatedly calling seek().\n")
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui animated-parts recipe: swarm.iteration/v1 sections and managed temporal parts describe targetable time ranges; they do not animate the document. Declare one canonical iteration manifest whose ordered non-overlapping sections cover the animation timeline, and submit one managed temporal part per section with the exact same id, label, start_ms, and end_ms. Section selection may seek to a range, but ordinary preview playback must still come from the artifact-owned scheduler.\n")
		b.WriteString("- motion_ui Artifact Studio bridge recipe: install the swarm-player/v1 message listener before DOMContentLoaded so an early preview handshake cannot be missed. For every supported request, reply with the canonical envelope {protocol: 'swarm-player/v1', id: request.id, ok: true, result}; never substitute request_id, type, or a top-level manifest. A describe request must ensure initialization is complete, return the exact swarm.iteration/v1 manifest in result, and resume the one live RAF scheduler when the document is visible and reduced motion is not requested. This resume is mandatory because Studio may send stop immediately before describe on first load. seek and stop must acknowledge through the same envelope and retain their deterministic pause/freeze behavior. In-artifact section buttons and active states must use the same manifest boundaries and shared renderAt timeline as the managed temporal parts.\n")
	case "spatial_3d":
		b.WriteString("- Three.js authoring contract: the pinned Three.js runtime is an ES module supplied through Swarm's trusted import map. Use a nonce-preserved `<script type=\"module\">` and import from the bare specifier `three`; never use a CDN URL or remote import. Reuse geometries, materials, textures, vectors, and render targets; prefer instancing for repeated meshes; compile and load before the hot loop; resize only when the display size changes; cap renderer pixel ratio to the profile budget; and dispose every GPU resource on teardown.\n")
	case "vector_playback":
		b.WriteString("- vector_playback performance tips: use the pinned player's native timeline instead of a second JavaScript RAF or polling loop, keep imported artboard/layer/path complexity bounded, avoid duplicated live players, and stop and destroy the player when hidden, offscreen, or unmounted.\n")
	case "final_render":
		b.WriteString("- final_render performance tips: use native MP4 playback rather than recreating rendered motion with live DOM, Canvas, or WebGL work; provide an intentional poster/static first frame and release playback resources when hidden, offscreen, or unmounted.\n")
	}
}
